var tafsirData_31 = {
  "1": "The surah opens with the three separate Arabic letters Alif, Lām, and Mīm, a pattern found at the beginning of several chapters of the Quran. These are known as the disconnected letters, and their precise meaning remains a matter of the unseen known fully only to Allah.\n\nYet their placement here is profoundly meaningful. They immediately capture attention and remind the listener that this revelation is composed of the very letters of the Arabic language, the language of the Arabs who prided themselves on eloquence. Despite possessing these same letters, no one could produce anything like the Quran.\n\nThus the letters serve as a silent challenge, demonstrating that while the tools are ordinary, the resulting Book is miraculous. When recited aloud, Alif-Lām-Mīm introduces a tone of reverence and contemplation, preparing the heart for what follows. Many companions and early scholars also noted that the disconnected letters function as names for the Quran itself, or as oaths by which Allah swears by the building blocks of His speech.\n\nIn this surah, they introduce a message rich in wisdom, directing attention toward Luqmān’s advice and the broader themes of gratitude, monotheism, and accountability. The mystery of these letters reminds us that not all knowledge is accessible to the human mind and that humility before divine wisdom is the foundation of faith. Accepting that some matters belong to Allah alone keeps the believer grounded, never arrogantly assuming the intellect can uncover everything. So the very first verse sets the stage: listen with an open heart, for the words that follow are not ordinary speech but a wise and merciful guide.",
  "2": "After the opening letters, Allah immediately describes the revelation as the verses of the Book, meaning the Quran, which is characterised as being rich in wisdom. The word used for wisdom in Arabic is ḥakīm, which also conveys the sense of something made perfectly precise, well-constructed, and containing profound judgment.\n\nThis description tells us that the Quran’s teachings are not merely rules or stories, but every word is placed with deep insight and purpose, aiming to correct human thought and behavior. Wisdom here implies that the Book distinguishes truth from falsehood, places everything in its right context, and leads to the most beneficial outcome for those who follow it. The verses are living guidance that helps a person navigate life’s complexities, addressing both inner purification and outward conduct. The fact that the Book itself is called wise also indicates that its source is the All-Wise, for only a wise Creator can produce such perfectly measured revelation.\n\nThis description comforts the believers, assuring them that the path they tread is not based on conjecture but on divine wisdom that sees the past, present, and future simultaneously. Every prohibition, permission, story, and parable is an expression of that wisdom. For the skeptic who hears “Alif-Lām-Mīm,” this verse answers the natural question: what are these letters? They are the building blocks of a wise scripture. The connection between the two verses invites us to move from the recognition of the letters to the appreciation of the profound message they form, a message that will later unfold in the counsel of Luqmān and the descriptions of creation that follow.",
  "3": "The Quran is then further qualified as a guide and a mercy, but the verse restricts this benefit to the good-doers, those known as al-muḥsinīn. This description is crucial because it shows that while the Quran is potentially a source of guidance for all, only those who sincerely incline towards goodness and righteousness will truly benefit from its light.\n\nThe word hudan, meaning guidance, indicates that the Book leads humanity out of the darkness of ignorance, doubt, and misguidance into the clarity of faith. The word raḥmah, mercy, reveals that the teachings are not a burden but a compassionate gift, bringing peace and salvation in this life and the Hereafter. By specifying the good-doers, the verse teaches that a person’s receptivity matters. Someone who already possesses the quality of iḥsān—the desire to do what is beautiful and excellent—will find in the Quran the direction and the merciful healing they seek. This does not mean the Quran is exclusive, but that a closed, arrogant heart cannot extract its benefits. Throughout the Quran, Allah describes various human responses; here, the focus is positive, setting up the description of those who establish prayer and pay zakāh. The early Meccan context is vital, as the believers were a small group facing rejection and hardship. This verse assured them that the divine words were personally tailored as a mercy for them, strengthening their resolve. It also invites every listener to examine their own heart: are they among the good-doers whom the Quran will guide and envelop in mercy, or are they blocking themselves from receiving this gift?",
  "4": "The good-doers mentioned in the previous verse are now identified by three defining qualities. First, they establish prayer, meaning they perform the ṣalāh regularly, correctly, and with inward devotion, not merely going through motions.\n\nThe use of the word “establish” (yuqīmūn) indicates that they make prayer a firm pillar in their lives, observing its conditions, timings, and spiritual concentration. This was a central act of worship even in Mecca before the full five daily prayers were prescribed during the Night Journey. Second, they pay the alms-tax, the zakāh, which purifies their wealth and souls by giving a portion to those in need. This cultivates compassion, reduces greed, and builds a community of mutual care. Third, they have sure faith in the Hereafter; the Arabic yaqīn conveys absolute certainty, not mere hope. They live with a clear awareness that this world is temporary and that they will stand before their Lord for judgment. This certainty naturally affects their decisions, making them prioritize eternal outcomes over fleeting pleasures. Together, these three traits cover a person’s relationship with Allah (prayer), relationship with creation (charity), and inner conviction about the ultimate reality (faith in the Hereafter).\n\nThe verse presents a balanced picture of faith that is neither purely ritualistic nor purely intellectual; it manifests in bodily worship, financial sacrifice, and a mindset shaped by the unseen. This balance is the wisdom of the Book in action, preparing the reader for the later advice of Luqmān to his son, where prayer and humility feature prominently.",
  "5": "Having described their qualities, Allah now declares the outcome for such people. They are truly guided by their Lord, which means they are upon a clear path that originates from Allah Himself. The guidance is not self-generated; it is a divine gift bestowed upon those who show sincerity and effort.\n\nThe repetition of “it is they” emphasizes exclusivity, distinguishing the true recipients of guidance from those who merely claim belief. Then comes the promise of success, falāḥ, a comprehensive term in the Quran that encompasses salvation from Hell, entry into Paradise, and a state of flourishing in both worlds. The successful ones are not necessarily the wealthiest or most powerful by worldly standards, but they achieve the ultimate triumph that every soul deep down desires. This verse reassures the struggling Meccan believers that despite their minority status and persecution, they are the ones on the right track, and their future is secure. It also sets up a contrast with the following verses about those who trade guidance for misguidance. The structure of the surah carefully establishes a model of true success before exposing its opposite, so that the listener can clearly see the two paths. The link to Luqmān’s story is subtle: Luqmān was also guided by his Lord through wisdom, and his success lay in gratitude and good counsel. The early Muslims could see their own reflection in this verse, and every generation after them can measure themselves against these standards, asking whether they truly embody the traits that lead to divine guidance and ultimate success.",
  "6": "After the portrait of the sincere good-doers, the surah turns to a contrasting group: those who use idle tales, or theatrics, to mislead people from the path of Allah. The Arabic term lahw al-ḥadīth refers to diverting speech or entertainment that captures attention and distracts from what truly matters. Historical reports, including narrations from Ibn ʿAbbās and others, indicate that this verse was revealed concerning a man like Al-Naḍr ibn al-Ḥārith, who would travel to Persia, purchase books of legends and fables, and then recite them to the Quraysh, boasting that his stories were more entertaining than the Quran. He deliberately used captivating narratives to draw people away from listening to revelation.\n\nThe phrase “without any knowledge” exposes his ignorance; he had no real understanding of divine truth, yet he arrogantly set himself up as an alternative guide. Moreover, he made a mockery of Allah’s Way, ridiculing the message and those who followed it. The verse warns that such actions invite a humiliating punishment, one that brings disgrace because their arrogance and mockery will be met with severe, debasing torment in the Hereafter. This serves as a timeless warning against the misuse of media, entertainment, and storytelling to undermine faith. Even today, some forms of content may not openly attack religion but simply consume so much time and attention that people drift away from spiritual growth and reflection. The punishment is described as humiliating because they sought honor and attention through their theatrics, so they will be recompensed with the opposite. This verse bridges the earlier praise of the believers with the ensuing emphasis on revelation and gratitude, showing the consequences of treating divine guidance lightly.",
  "7": "This verse describes the reaction of the same people when the Quranic verses are recited. They turn away with an arrogance that is not just physical but deeply psychological. The comparison to deafness is striking: they behave as if they did not hear the words at all, not because their ears malfunction, but because their hearts are blocked by pride.\n\nThe phrase “as if there is deafness in their ears” employs a powerful simile; outwardly their hearing is intact, but the truth does not penetrate. It is a chosen deafness, a refusal to engage sincerely. The command to the Prophet, “So give them good news of a painful punishment,” uses irony to deliver a severe warning. Normally, “good news” conveys glad tidings, but here it is the announcement of what awaits them. This rhetorical device jolts the listener, making them realize the gravity of the situation. The painful punishment is a direct consequence of their turning away and mockery mentioned in the previous verse.\n\nThe sequence shows that rejecting divine signs after they have been clearly presented is not a neutral act; it invites calamity. For the believers enduring taunts, this verse provided reassurance that justice would ultimately be served. For all readers, it serves as a sobering reminder to approach the Quran with humility and openness, lest a hardness of heart render one spiritually deaf. The connection to Luqmān’s advice is evident later: he counsels his son to be humble in posture and voice, the exact opposite of the arrogance displayed here.",
  "8": "In stark contrast to the humiliating punishment of the mockers, the surah returns to the reward of the faithful. The opening word “surely” (inna) conveys certainty and emphasis. The description “those who believe and do good” pairs inner conviction with outward righteous deeds, a recurring Quranic principle that faith must be accompanied by action to bear fruit. The promised reward is the Gardens of Bliss, jannāt al-naʿīm, which denote gardens of perpetual delight, comfort, and joy where every desire is fulfilled and no hardship exists.\n\nThe word “naʿīm” specifically evokes ease, luxury, and pleasure, painting a picture that contrasts powerfully with the painful punishment mentioned just before. This alternating pattern of warning and glad tidings is characteristic of the Quran’s method: it shakes the heart with fear of punishment and then soothes it with hope of reward, so that the believer lives between hope and fear. The believers in Mecca, who faced social ostracism and economic boycott, could lift their eyes to this promise, knowing that their struggles were temporary and that eternal bliss awaited them.\n\nThe mention of “Gardens” also connects to the imagery of Paradise throughout the Quran, where lush greenery, flowing rivers, and cool shade represent the ultimate homecoming. This verse prepares the ground for the subsequent affirmation that Allah’s promise is true, reinforcing that the believers’ hope is not a wishful fantasy but a guaranteed reality from the Almighty, All-Wise. It is a direct encouragement to continue in their faith and good works, no matter the opposition.",
  "9": "The description of the reward continues with the crucial detail that the believers will abide in those gardens eternally. The permanence of Paradise removes any fear of loss or ending, which magnifies the bliss infinitely. A temporary pleasure, however intense, is always shadowed by the sorrow of its cessation, but eternal bliss is complete joy.\n\nThe verse then grounds this promise in the truthfulness of Allah: “Allah’s promise is true. ” This short sentence is a profound anchor for the believer. Human beings may break promises, but the Creator, free from all deficiency, never fails in His word. The two concluding divine names, the Almighty and the All-Wise, are particularly fitting. He is Almighty, so He has absolute power to fulfill this promise; no force can prevent Him from admitting the believers to Paradise and granting them everlasting life. He is All-Wise, so this promise is made with perfect knowledge and justice, not arbitrarily.\n\nThe pairing of might and wisdom assures that Paradise is not a wishful myth but the deliberate, powerful decree of the One who governs all existence. This verse completes the portrait of the believers’ destination that began in verse 8, offering a profound sense of security. It also subtly links back to the opening verses: the Book is rich in wisdom, and the One who revealed it is All-Wise, ensuring that its glad tidings are not empty words but a binding covenant.\n\nFor those who suffer because of their faith, meditating on this verse renews patience and hope, reminding them that their Lord’s promise is unbreakable.",
  "10": "The focus shifts from the Hereafter to the visible signs of Allah’s power and wisdom in creation. The verse begins by drawing attention to the heavens, which are raised without visible pillars.\n\nThe phrase “as you can see” engages the observer directly, inviting reflection on the sky that stretches above, held by invisible divine command. In a time with no telescopes, the vast, unsupported expanse was a clear sign of the Creator’s ability. Then the verse moves to the earth, where firm mountains are described as stabilizers, preventing the earth from shaking violently with its inhabitants. Modern geology acknowledges mountains’ deep roots, but the primary point is that Allah’s design ensures a stable habitat. Next, the scattering of all types of creatures indicates the diversity of life—animals, birds, insects—each a testament to creative brilliance.\n\nThe final part mentions rain descending from the sky, causing the growth of every kind of fine, pleasing plant. The Arabic “zawj karīm” suggests plants that are noble, beneficial, and beautiful, providing food and delight.\n\nThis sequence—heavens, earth, creatures, plants—is a concise yet comprehensive tour of the cosmos, emphasizing that everything is deliberately created and sustained. The verse prepares for the rhetorical question that follows, challenging the idolaters to show what their false gods have created. It also connects deeply with Luqmān’s later advice to his son to be aware that Allah sees all things, for the same Creator who established these massive systems is intimately aware of every tiny deed.",
  "11": "After listing the wonders of creation, Allah delivers a direct challenge to the polytheists. “This is Allah’s creation” is a simple, undeniable statement. The heavens, the mountains, the creatures, and the plants are all observable realities. The command “Now show Me what those other than Him have created” is a rhetorical demand that exposes the emptiness of idol worship. The idols, whether stone statues, celestial bodies, or ancestral spirits, have created nothing—not even a fly. This challenge invites rational reflection: if these beings cannot create anything, how can they be worthy of worship?\n\nThe verse then concludes with a statement of fact: the wrongdoers are clearly astray. Their misguidance is not hidden or excusable; it is evident in the light of creation’s clear testimony. This argument appears repeatedly in the Quran, such as in Surah Al-Ḥajj where Allah challenges the idols to create a fly. The Meccan pagans themselves acknowledged Allah as the Creator of the heavens and earth, a point made later in this very surah. Their contradiction was obvious, and this verse exposes it with unassailable logic. This rhetorical style serves as a powerful tool for believers in any era to defend monotheism. It teaches that faith is not blind; it is rooted in observation and reason, guided by revelation. The transition to Luqmān’s story is now seamless: he, as a wise man, recognised this truth and imparted it to his son, beginning with the most fundamental command: never associate partners with Allah. The stage is set for a living example of wisdom responding to creation’s signs.",
  "12": "With the evidence of creation established, the surah introduces Luqmān, a figure renowned for his wisdom. The majority of scholars hold that Luqmān was a righteous man, not a prophet, possibly of African descent, known for his sound judgment and insightful sayings. Allah says He blessed him with wisdom, and that wisdom is immediately linked to gratitude. The command “Be grateful to Allah” is the first expression of that wisdom. Gratitude is presented as the proper human response to the countless favors mentioned in the previous verses.\n\nThe verse then clarifies a central principle: gratitude benefits only the grateful person. Allah is in no need of thanks; the act of being grateful elevates the soul, increases blessings, and brings contentment. Conversely, ingratitude does not harm Allah at all, for He is Self-Sufficient and Praiseworthy. He is completely free of any need, so human rebellion diminishes nothing from His majesty, and He is inherently deserving of praise regardless of whether people acknowledge it.\n\nThis teaching dismantles the arrogance of those who withhold gratitude as if they are withholding something from God. Wisdom, therefore, begins with recognizing one’s total dependence on the Creator and responding with heartfelt thanks. This aligns with the earlier depiction of the good-doers, who are grateful servants.\n\nThe mention of Luqmān’s wisdom sets the stage for the series of counsels he gave his son, which are not just paternal advice but divinely approved gems that the Quran records for all generations.",
  "13": "The Quran now relays the actual words of Luqmān’s counsel, beginning with the most critical matter: pure monotheism. The affectionate address, “O my dear son,” shows the loving, concerned tone of a father who wants the best for his child. The prohibition is against shirk, associating partners with Allah in worship, belief, or lordship. Luqmān emphasizes this because it is the foundation upon which all other deeds rest. The verse explains why: associating others with Allah is a grave wrong, in fact the greatest wrongdoing.\n\nThe Arabic term “ẓulm” means placing something in its wrong place, and giving worship, which belongs exclusively to the Creator, to created things is the ultimate injustice. This wrong is not merely against oneself; it is a transgression against the rights of Allah, against one’s own soul, and against the truth of existence. The warning against shirk is the first and most emphatic point because if a person’s concept of God is corrupted, every other aspect of life becomes distorted. Luqmān’s wisdom aligns perfectly with the message of every prophet, showing that monotheism is the natural, rational response to the signs of creation described earlier. The heavens and earth without pillars, the mountains, and the plants all point to One Creator; therefore, turning to idols or other beings is absurd and deeply wrong. This verse serves as the headline of Luqmān’s advice, with the subsequent counsels flowing from the root of true faith. It also reminds parents of their prime responsibility: to nurture sound belief in their children with love and clarity.",
  "14": "Having recorded Luqmān’s advice against shirk, the Quran now interweaves a direct divine command concerning parents, creating a beautiful link. Tawḥīd is followed by kindness to parents, a pairing found throughout the Quran. The command to honor parents is universal, but the verse singles out the mother for special mention, highlighting her unique sacrifices: she bore the child through hardship upon hardship, from pregnancy’s physical strain to the pains of labor, and then nursed the child for up to two years. This detailed mention of pregnancy and weaning appeals to the conscience, leaving no doubt about the immense debt a child owes the mother. The command to be grateful to Allah and to parents places parental gratitude right after divine gratitude, elevating the status of parents, especially the mother.\n\nYet this gratitude has a condition, as the next verse clarifies. The final sentence, “To Me is the final return,” serves as a gentle reminder that this relationship is accountable; the child will answer to Allah for how they treated their parents, and the parents will answer for how they raised their child. It also consoles children who may suffer from difficult parents, for ultimate justice lies with Allah. This verse echoes Luqmān’s own advice and frames it within Allah’s own speech, showing that Luqmān’s wisdom was divinely inspired. It prepares for the exception that follows, ensuring that obedience to parents never overrides obedience to the Creator.",
  "15": "The command to honor parents is immediately balanced with a crucial boundary. If parents strive, with utmost effort, to force their child into shirk—associating with Allah something for which there is no proof or knowledge—the child must not obey them in that specific matter.\n\nThe phrase “what you have no knowledge of” underlines that shirk is baseless; there is no sound evidence for it, only blind imitation. Yet even in this conflict, the child is not allowed to sever ties or treat them harshly. The instruction is clear: still keep their company in this world courteously. That means maintaining kind speech, financial support if needed, and gentle companionship, all while firmly refusing to compromise on faith.\n\nThis is a profound balance that many struggle with. The verse then instructs to follow the way of those who turn to Allah, meaning the path of the prophets and righteous believers, the community of faith that reinforces monotheism.\n\nThe final sentence brings back the theme of accountability: all will return to Allah, and He will inform them of the true nature of their deeds. This comforts the believing child who faced pressure, knowing that Allah saw their struggle and will reward their patience. It also warns the misguided parents that their coercion will not go unjudged. This verse directly connects to the core theme of gratitude and warns against the blind following of ancestors, a topic picked up later in the surah.",
  "16": "The surah returns to Luqmān’s words to his son, now focusing on Allah’s all-encompassing knowledge and justice. The image is breathtakingly precise: a deed as light as a mustard seed, the proverbial smallest weight. Even if that tiny deed were hidden inside a solid rock, or lost somewhere in the vastness of the heavens, or buried deep within the earth, Allah will bring it forth on the Day of Judgment.\n\nThis statement leaves no room for any sin or good act to be overlooked. The Prophet Muhammad confirmed this concept in a well-known narration describing that on the Day of Resurrection, even the smallest good and evil will be presented. The two divine names ending the verse are perfectly matched: Al-Laṭīf, the Most Subtle, indicates that Allah’s knowledge penetrates the most hidden, fine, and inaccessible matters without any difficulty, while Al-Khabīr, the All-Aware, signifies comprehensive awareness of every detail and inner reality. Together they assure that divine judgment is based on perfect, unobstructed knowledge. For a young person, this teaching instills a profound sense of personal accountability; one cannot hide from Allah, not in the darkest cave nor the farthest galaxy. This awareness naturally encourages sincerity, because even a private act of charity or a secret sinful thought is fully known and will be brought to light. The lesson links back to the creation verses: the One who could create heavens without pillars and scatter creatures throughout the earth can surely keep track of every atom of human action.\n\nThis principle shapes moral behavior when no human eye is watching.",
  "17": "With the foundation of faith and awareness of accountability firmly laid, Luqmān moves to the practical pillars of righteous living. First, establish prayer, making it a constant, properly performed act of worship. The same word “establish” used earlier for the believers shows consistency. Prayer is the daily renewal of one’s connection with Allah, and it fuels the strength to carry out the next commands. Second, encourage what is good and forbid what is evil. This elevates the son’s role from personal piety to social responsibility; a true believer is not passive but actively promotes virtue and gently corrects wrongdoing in the community.\n\nThe sequence is important: one must first be grounded in prayer before being able to effectively enjoin good, because the task is demanding and requires spiritual energy. Third, endure patiently whatever befalls you. Fulfilling these duties invites opposition, criticism, and hardship, so patience is essential.\n\nThe word “ṣabr” implies steadfastness without complaint. The concluding phrase “surely this is a resolve to aspire to” means these matters are among the great, firm resolutions that Allah commands, the kind of determination that separates the strong in faith from the weak. They are not optional extras but essential goals that require serious commitment. This advice encapsulates the core of the believer’s mission: maintain your link with Allah, reform society, and hold steady through trials. It resonates deeply with the Meccan context where the Prophet and his followers were patiently persevering in the face of persecution while calling people to monotheism.",
  "18": "Luqmān’s counsel now addresses inner character and external behavior, specifically the disease of pride. The first prohibition is against turning one’s cheek away from people in scorn, a gesture of contempt and superiority.\n\nThe second is walking upon the earth with an exaggerated, proud gait, as if to demonstrate one’s importance. Together, these cover the attitude of the heart and the demeanor of the body. The verse explains the reason: Allah does not love every arrogant, boastful person.\n\nThe Arabic “mukhtāl fakhūr” describes someone who is deluded by self-admiration and constantly boasts about their qualities, lineage, or achievements. This is the opposite of the humility that faith demands. Arrogance was the sin of Iblīs, who refused to bow to Adam, and it is the trait that often keeps people from accepting the truth. The verse connects to the earlier description of those who turned away in arrogance from the Quran’s recitation. True wisdom, as Luqmān teaches, produces modesty. Knowing that all blessings come from Allah and that every human being is created from humble origins cures pride.\n\nMoreover, the prohibition of boastfulness reminds a person that any good they possess is a gift from Allah, not a personal accomplishment to flaunt. In daily life, this translates to treating everyone with respect, walking modestly, and maintaining a quiet confidence that does not seek to belittle others.\n\nThe following verse extends this theme of moderation into the realm of voice and gait.",
  "19": "This verse continues the theme of humility and dignified conduct, now focusing on moderation in walking and lowering one’s voice. The instruction to be moderate in pace means not to walk too slowly in a manner of arrogance, nor too hastily in a restless, undignified way, but to adopt a balanced, purposeful gait. This teaches that even a mundane action like walking can be an act of worship when done mindfully. The command to lower the voice aims at avoiding loud, harsh, or boisterous speech. It includes refraining from shouting unnecessarily, raising the voice in argument beyond need, or speaking in a tone that intimidates or annoys others. The vivid comparison that follows is striking: the most repulsive of voices is the braying of a donkey. This powerful simile leaves a strong impression; a raised, harsh voice is compared to an animal known for its unpleasant, loud sound. The wisdom behind this is that loudness often stems from arrogance, anger, or a desire to dominate, all of which are unworthy of a believer. Soft speech reflects a calm heart and respect for others. The Prophet was known for his gentle, measured speech, never raising his voice indecently. This advice encompasses everything from table talk to public discourse, encouraging an atmosphere of peace and mutual respect. Both verses 18 and 19 provide a practical code of social conduct that shapes a personality beloved to Allah and to people. They complete Luqmān’s recorded advice, leaving a timeless manual for raising children with wisdom and love.",
  "20": "After the intimate counsel of Luqmān, the discourse returns to a direct address to humanity at large, calling attention to Allah’s universal favors. The rhetorical question, “Have you not seen,” invites reflection on the observable world. Everything in the heavens—the sun, moon, stars, and clouds—and everything on earth—the animals, rivers, and vegetation—has been made subservient to human beings, meaning it is designed to benefit them. This subjection is not by human power but by divine decree, a gift that should inspire awe and gratitude.\n\nThe verse then mentions that Allah has lavished His favors in abundance, both those that are seen, like health, wealth, and nature’s beauty, and those that are unseen, like faith, intellect, safety, and divine protection. The word “lavished” implies overflowing generosity that far exceeds basic needs.\n\nYet despite this overwhelming kindness, some people still dispute about Allah’s oneness, power, or the truth of revelation. They do so without any foundation: no knowledge, no genuine guidance, and no enlightening scripture. This threefold negation demolishes their position entirely; they argue from ignorance, follow no reliable source of guidance, and possess no revealed book to support their claims. The contrast between Allah’s tangible favors and their baseless disputation exposes the unreasonableness of ingratitude and denial. This verse bridges the grateful wisdom of Luqmān with the stubbornness of the polytheists, setting up the next verse’s critique of blind ancestral adherence. The pattern is clear: abundant signs call for humble acceptance, not empty argument.",
  "21": "Building directly on the end of the previous verse, which condemned those who dispute about Allah without knowledge, this verse exposes the root of their stubbornness: blind adherence to ancestral tradition. When the polytheists are invited with a simple, clear call to “Follow what Allah has revealed,” meaning the Quran and the message of pure monotheism, their immediate reaction is a flat refusal. They do not engage with the evidence, nor do they ask for clarification. Instead, they retreat into the familiar comfort of their forefathers’ ways, declaring, “We only follow what we found our forefathers practicing. ” This reply reveals a mindset that equates truth with heritage, as if the passage of time alone sanctifies belief. The Quran challenges this by posing a devastating rhetorical question: would they still cling to those inherited practices even if, in reality, Satan is the one inviting them through that legacy to the torment of the blazing Fire?\n\nThe question implies that ancestral customs, when they contradict divine revelation, are not neutral but actually become tools in Satan’s hand. Satan beautifies the old ways, making them seem noble tradition, while their ultimate end is destruction. This verse cuts to the heart of a human tendency found in every age: the unquestioning veneration of the past. It reminds us that the truth is not determined by how many generations held a belief, but by whether it aligns with what Allah has revealed. The early Meccan Muslims faced immense pressure to abandon their faith and return to the religion of their fathers; this verse armed them with a rational and spiritual rebuttal. It also teaches that true respect for ancestors means wanting for them the truth, not blindly copying their errors. The logic is simple and piercing: if a path leads to hellfire, then following it is madness, no matter who walked it before.",
  "22": "In contrast to those who follow their forefathers into error, this verse presents the portrait of the truly successful person. The phrase “fully submits themselves to Allah” translates the Arabic yuslim wajhahu ilā Allāh, which literally means to turn one’s face entirely towards Allah in surrender. This implies complete devotion, directing one’s entire being—intentions, desires, and actions—to the Creator alone. This submission is paired with being a good-doer, muhsin, meaning one who performs deeds with excellence and sincerity, just as described in the early verses of the surah. The result of such a comprehensive Islamic posture is that the person has “grasped the firmest hand-hold,” al-ʿurwah al-wuthqā.\n\nThis is a powerful metaphor: imagine a climber who finds an unbreakable, secure grip on a solid rock, ensuring they will not fall. That hand-hold is the testimony of faith, the commitment to tawḥīd, and the life of obedience built upon it. It is the most trustworthy anchor in a world of shifting sands.\n\nThe verse then closes with a profound reassurance: “And with Allah rests the outcome of all affairs.” No matter how chaotic events seem, the final resolution and judgment of every matter belong to Allah alone. This connects beautifully with the previous counsel of Luqmān, who rooted his son’s character in submission and patience. The believers who hear this are reminded that their grip on faith is their safety, and whatever temporary victories the disbelievers seem to enjoy, the ultimate outcome is with their Lord. The verse balances personal responsibility—submitting and doing good—with trust in divine decree, offering both a call to action and a deep sense of peace.",
  "23": "Having described the secure condition of the believer, the surah turns to console the Prophet Muhammad directly, and by extension every caller to truth, regarding those who reject faith. The human heart naturally feels sorrow when people reject guidance, especially when they are beloved community members. The Prophet’s deep concern for his people was well known, and this verse lifts that emotional burden. The command “do not let their disbelief grieve you” is a divine balm, releasing the messenger from a weight he was never meant to carry alone. The reason follows: their final destination is not in the caller’s hands but “to Us is their return.” Allah will handle the reckoning. He will inform them of everything they did, with a knowledge so complete that it encompasses even the most hidden secrets of the heart.\n\nThe verse ends by affirming that Allah knows best what is in the chests, the inner recesses of thought, motive, and feeling that no human can access. This serves both as a warning to the disbelievers—they cannot hide their stubbornness or insincerity—and as a comfort to the believer, knowing that the injustice and mockery they suffered are fully recorded.\n\nThis principle is echoed in many places in the Quran, reinforcing that the ultimate role of a prophet and his followers is to convey, not to compel. The lesson for all who invite others to faith is profound: care deeply, but do not allow the rejection to consume you. Your task is the delivery; the accounting belongs to the All-Knowing. The flow from verse 22 is clear: those who submit grasp the firmest handhold; those who do not are in God’s hands for judgment, not yours.",
  "24": "This verse continues the address about the disbelievers, explaining the apparent delay in their punishment. The worldly prosperity or pleasure that the rejecters enjoy is a brief respite granted by Allah.\n\nThe phrase “a little while” emphasizes the fleeting nature of this life compared to eternity; all the wealth, power, and amusement that distract them will vanish. The verb “We allow them enjoyment” indicates that even this temporary enjoyment is by divine permission, not by their own power, and it serves as a test. Then, when the appointed term ends, the tone shifts dramatically. The verse says “We will force them” using a word that conveys being driven or compelled, eliminating any possibility of escape. The torment described as “harsh” is severe, unrelenting, and painful, the direct opposite of their prior ease. This juxtaposition of a short, pleasant interval against an overwhelming, forced punishment serves as a stark warning. It reminds believers not to envy the temporary comforts of those who reject faith, for those comforts are like the small, sweet bait that hides a deadly trap. The knowledge that the punishment is harsh and inevitable gives the oppressed community patience. It also answers a perennial question: why do the wicked sometimes seem to prosper? The Quran’s answer is consistent—it is merely a brief extension that only increases their ultimate ruin, a principle stated elsewhere that Allah grants respite so that they may increase in sin and thus deserve a heavier recompense. The verse thus links to the previous consolation: the Prophet should not grieve because their enjoyment is fleeting, and a just and severe outcome awaits.",
  "25": "The surah now exposes a glaring contradiction in the pagans’ belief system. Even the Meccan idolaters, if asked directly about the ultimate origin of the heavens and the earth, would immediately answer “Allah.” They recognized that the supreme Creator was Allah alone, and their idols were only intermediaries. This acknowledgement is presented as a rhetorical point: their own admission establishes that the true Creator and Lord is Allah. The instruction to the Prophet is to say “Al-ḥamdu lillāh,” all praise belongs to Allah, a phrase that here serves as an expression of gratitude for this residual truth and as a declaration of Allah’s uniqueness.\n\nYet the verse immediately follows with a sharp observation: “In fact, most of them do not know.” They possess the raw knowledge, but they fail to grasp its implications. If Allah is the Creator of all things, then worship, devotion, and absolute obedience must be directed to Him alone. Their knowledge is superficial because it does not translate into proper action and pure monotheism.\n\nThis is a crucial insight: knowledge that does not lead to correct practice is incomplete and effectively a form of ignorance. The verse ties back to Luqmān’s first counsel against shirk, showing that shirk is often rooted in a failure to follow logic to its conclusion. The argument invites the listener to complete the reasoning: if Allah created everything, what have the idols created? That challenge was already laid out in verse 11. Here, the pagans’ own testimony is used against them, a method that demonstrates the power of engaging people with their own stated beliefs. It also highlights that true faith is not just verbal acknowledgment but a wholehearted acceptance that shapes one’s life.",
  "26": "After recording the pagans’ partial admission that Allah is the Creator, this verse declares the full and absolute ownership of Allah over everything that exists in the heavens and the earth. The statement “to Allah belongs whatever is in the heavens and the earth” is comprehensive, covering all beings, objects, resources, and even the idols the pagans worship, since those idols are mere objects within Allah’s creation. This ownership is not merely legal; it is the complete dominion of the Creator who sustains, controls, and governs all.\n\nThe verse then closes with two divine names: Al-Ghanī, the Self-Sufficient, and Al-Ḥamīd, the Praiseworthy. Allah is Self-Sufficient in that He has absolutely no need for any of His creation, nor for their worship or gratitude. The entire cosmos adds nothing to His majesty, and its absence would subtract nothing. Simultaneously, He is inherently Praiseworthy; all praise in the heavens and the earth ultimately returns to Him because every instance of beauty, goodness, and perfection is a reflection of His attributes or a gift from His hand. This pairing of names echoes verse 12, where Luqmān was told that whoever is ungrateful, Allah is Self-Sufficient, Praiseworthy.\n\nThe repetition reinforces the lesson: human ingratitude does not diminish Allah in any way. The verse also serves as a firm theological foundation against shirk. If Allah owns everything and is self-sufficient, then associating partners with Him is not only offensive but utterly irrational—what can a created idol give to the One who owns all and needs nothing? This truth invites the believer to worship Allah purely out of love and recognition of His perfection, not because He requires it.",
  "27": "This magnificent verse uses a striking visual metaphor to convey the limitless nature of Allah’s words, knowledge, and creative power. The image is of all the trees on the planet transformed into writing pens, and the vast ocean turned into ink, and then that ocean being replenished by seven more oceans, an Arabic expression indicating infinite multiplication. Even with such an unimaginable supply of writing tools and ink, the Words of Allah would still not run out.\n\nThe term “Words of Allah” refers to His speech, His knowledge, His decrees, and the manifestations of His creative command. Every leaf, every galaxy, every law of nature is a word from Him. As one hadith explains, if the oceans were ink for the words of my Lord, the ocean would be depleted before the words of my Lord are exhausted, even if we brought similar oceans to replenish them.\n\nThe verse concludes by affirming that Allah is Almighty, so He can actualize His words without limitation, and All-Wise, so His words are full of perfect wisdom. This not only expands the believer’s sense of awe but also answers a potential objection: how could the Quran, a finite book, encompass all guidance? The Quran is a selection from Allah’s infinite knowledge suited to humanity’s needs.\n\nThe verse also connects to the earlier description of the Book as “rich in wisdom”; its wisdom flows from an infinite source. The lesson for the believer is humility before the vastness of divine knowledge. We can never fully wrap our minds around all of Allah’s wisdom, but we trust that what He has revealed is sufficient.\n\nThis verse also serves as a rebuttal to those who demand more signs; even if all creation were ink, it could not capture all the signs Allah could give.",
  "28": "After establishing the vastness of Allah’s words and knowledge, the surah addresses another common doubt: the resurrection of the countless dead across all ages. The verse draws a powerful analogy to demonstrate Allah’s absolute power. The creation of all of humanity and their subsequent resurrection is, for Allah, exactly like the creation and resurrection of a single soul.\n\nThe phrase “as simple as” reflects the Arabic illā ka-nafsin wāḥidah, meaning it is only like one soul. This demolishes the idea that a large number of beings poses any difficulty for the Creator. Human manufacturing and effort multiply with quantity, but divine power faces no such constraints. When Allah wills a thing, He merely says “Be,” and it is, whether one soul or billions.\n\nThe verse then links this immense power to two divine attributes: All-Hearing and All-Seeing. Allah hears every voice that calls upon Him and sees every scattered particle of the decayed bodies in the earth. Nothing escapes His perception, so the reassembly of every individual is fully within His knowledge and capability.\n\nThis is both a comfort for the believers awaiting justice and a warning for those who deny the resurrection. The Meccan pagans often mocked the idea that bones turned to dust could be brought back to life; this verse removes the perceived impossibility by pointing to the One whose power is not limited by scale. It also subtly ties back to Luqmān’s teaching that Allah will bring forth even a mustard seed’s weight of a deed; the One who can resurrect the entirety of humanity with a single command can surely account for the finest details of each person’s actions.",
  "29": "This verse directs the attention once more to the observable phenomena of the universe, calling the listener to reflect. The merging of night into day and day into night is a daily miracle. The transition is gradual, smooth, and continuous; the darkness slowly yields to light and vice versa, without a sudden cut. This gradual merging shows control, balance, and wisdom. The sun and the moon are described as being subjected, meaning they are compelled to follow the precise orbits and laws set for them. Their movement is not random but governed, and each runs for an appointed term—a predetermined span until the end of this cosmic order. This reminded the pagan Arabs, who worshipped the sun and moon in some cases, that these celestial bodies are not deities but servants of Allah, running their courses by His command.\n\nThe verse ends with a jarring personal note: “and that Allah is All-Aware of what you do. ” The shift from cosmic order to personal accountability is deliberate. The same meticulous precision that governs the heavens also governs the recording of human deeds. The One who maintains the orbits of the sun and moon does not neglect the actions of His servants. This link is meant to awaken a deep sense of being watched and to instill a reverence that leads to righteous conduct. The verse seamlessly connects natural signs with moral responsibility, a recurring Quranic pattern. It builds on the earlier creation verses, reinforcing that the cosmos is not a meaningless machine but a theatre of signs pointing to a purposeful, aware Creator.",
  "30": "This verse provides the underlying reason for the cosmic order and divine control just described. “That” refers to the reality of Allah’s management of the universe and His perfect awareness. The reason is rooted in the very nature of divinity: Allah alone is the Truth, the ultimate Reality whose existence is necessary and unchanging, while everything invoked besides Him is falsehood, bāṭil, meaning it is vanishing, ineffective, and unreal in its claim to divinity. The contrast is absolute and leaves no room for a middle ground. The idols, saints, or forces that people call upon have no independent existence or power; they are false, destined to perish.\n\nThe verse then reiterates two majestic attributes: Allah is the Most High, Al-ʿAlī, transcending all creation in His essence and authority, and the All-Great, Al-Kabīr, possessing supreme greatness that dwarfs everything. This declaration logically follows the preceding signs: only the True God could orchestrate the merging of night and day and the subjection of the sun and moon. False gods could never design or maintain such a system.\n\nThe verse also echoes the theme of Luqmān’s central advice to avoid shirk, because shirk is fundamentally an attachment to falsehood. By naming Allah as the Truth and everything else as falsehood, the surah draws a clear line that forces a decision. The human heart must cling to the One who is Real, or it will chase illusions that lead to ruin.\n\nFor the believer, this verse is a statement of profound security, anchoring the soul in the only reality that never fails.",
  "31": "Moving from the celestial signs to a maritime one, this verse directs attention to the ships that glide across the sea. The phrase “by the grace of Allah” points out that the smooth sailing is not merely a result of human engineering or favorable winds; it is a continuous divine favor. The water that could easily swallow the vessel is held in check, and the wind that could destroy it is made gentle, all by Allah’s mercy. The purpose is “so that He may show you some of His signs,” meaning that every successful voyage is a lived experience of divine care, a sign crafted from the fabric of daily life.\n\nThe verse then specifies that these signs are truly visible only to a particular type of person: every ṣabbār shakūr—whoever is steadfast and grateful. Steadfastness here implies patient endurance and consistency in reflecting and obeying, while gratitude indicates a heart that continually acknowledges the Giver. These two qualities form a complete response to life’s tests: patience in hardship and thankfulness in ease. When a person sails on a ship and reaches their destination safely, the grateful heart recognizes the sign and responds with praise, while the patient heart is reminded to trust Allah in all journeys.\n\nThe mention of ships is especially poignant for the Meccan audience, whose livelihood depended on trade caravans and maritime knowledge of others. The verse also foreshadows the next scene of a storm, where human nature is stripped to its core.\n\nThus the smooth sailing becomes both a gift and a test, preparing the soul for moments of crisis when the true nature of faith is revealed.",
  "32": "This verse paints a dramatic scene that exposes the raw human instinct for God. When a ship is surrounded by towering waves, described as being like mountains—an image of terrifying, overwhelming danger—the polytheists on board abandon their idols and cry out to Allah alone.\n\nThe phrase “in sincere devotion” translates the Arabic mukhliṣīn lahu al-dīn, meaning they purify their faith entirely for Him at that moment, forgetting all intermediaries. Their innate nature, the fiṭrah, surfaces when all worldly supports vanish. But the test comes after the rescue. When Allah delivers them safely to dry land, their response splits. The verse says “only some become relatively grateful,” meaning even those who show gratitude do so half-heartedly or temporarily, while the majority revert to their old ways.\n\nThe final statement declares a universal principle: none persistently and arrogantly rejects Allah’s signs except a person who is deeply treacherous, khattār, and habitually ungrateful, kafūr. The term khattār conveys constant betrayal and breaking of promises, describing someone who makes a pact with God in distress and then reneges once safe.\n\nThis is a powerful moral diagnosis. The storm at sea is not just a historical anecdote; it is a metaphor for the crises of life that reveal our true dependencies. The verse challenges the listener: will you be like the one who calls sincerely in trouble and then forgets, or will you be steadfast and grateful in all conditions? It ties back to Luqmān’s wisdom: gratitude must be constant, not circumstantial. The warning is clear—being among those described as deceitful and ungrateful leads to ruin.",
  "33": "After the parable of the sea, the surah issues a universal call to all humanity, marked by the direct address “O humanity.” The command is to have taqwā, a mindful consciousness of the Lord that leads to obedience and protection from His displeasure. The warning then focuses on the Day of Judgment, where the usual bonds of family, the strongest relationships in human life, will be completely severed in terms of intercession or help. No parent will be able to help their child, and no child will be able to help their parent in the slightest, unless Allah permits intercession for the believers. This shattering image dismantles the false security people place in family ties and tribal solidarity, which the Quraysh depended on heavily. The statement “Surely Allah’s promise is true” anchors the warning in certainty. Following this, two specific deceptions are identified against which humanity must be on guard. First, the life of this world must not deceive you, meaning its fleeting pleasures, distractions, and false sense of permanence should not lead one to neglect the eternal life. Second, the Chief Deceiver, al-gharūr, which refers to Satan, must not deceive you about Allah. Satan’s greatest trick is to make people feel secure in their sin by exploiting Allah’s mercy—“Allah will forgive you anyway”—or to make them despair of mercy altogether. Both are traps. This verse is a climactic warning that gathers the themes of the surah: gratitude, accountability, and the danger of self-deception. It serves as the final push before the closing verse on divine knowledge, reminding us to live with an eternal perspective.",
  "34": "The surah concludes with a verse that encompasses five keys of the unseen, matters of exclusive divine knowledge that no prophet or angel possesses independently. The Prophet Muhammad said in a well-known hadith reported by Al-Bukhari: “The keys of the unseen are five, and no one knows them but Allah,” and then he recited this very verse.\n\nThe first key is the knowledge of the Hour, the exact time of the Day of Judgment, which remains hidden so that every generation lives in readiness. The second is the sending down of rain: despite modern meteorology, the precise timing, location, and life-giving effect of rain are ultimately known only to Allah in their full detail and decree.\n\nThe third is knowledge of what is in the wombs, encompassing the complete development, gender, future traits, and destiny of the unborn child at every stage—far beyond what ultrasound images can reveal. The fourth key is that no soul knows what it will earn for tomorrow, meaning the future events, gains, losses, and specifically one’s deeds are entirely hidden. This demolishes any arrogance that assumes continued life and success. The fifth key is that no soul knows in what land it will die. The place and time of death are completely sealed from us, teaching humility and constant preparation.\n\nThe verse ends by affirming that Allah is All-Knowing, All-Aware, tying these exclusive domains to His perfect knowledge. This final message beautifully aligns with Luqmān’s advice to his son about Allah’s subtle, comprehensive knowledge. The surah that opened with the wise Book now closes by inviting the reader to rest in the One who alone knows the full picture, encouraging trust, humility, and readiness for the inevitable meeting with Him."
};
